 ]$$::jsonb
 || $$[
 {"id":"d1c-briefing","type":"prose","markdown":"## NASA Planetary Defense · Briefing 001\n\n**SUBJECT:** Near-Earth Object 2026-XJ — Initial Trajectory Assessment\n\n1. **DISCOVERY.** Object 2026-XJ was detected 11 days ago by the ATLAS survey. Follow-up observations from three stations confirm it is on an Earth-approaching trajectory.\n2. **CURRENT BEST DATA.** Distance to Earth: **1.2 × 10⁸ km** (± 50,000 km). Velocity: approximately **8,500 m/s** (± 200 m/s), directed 15° off the Earth-line. Mass estimate: **3.5 × 10¹¹ kg**, inferred from brightness — *low confidence*.\n3. **WHAT WE DO NOT KNOW.** Composition (rock, metal, or rubble). Rotation state. Whether its path will be significantly bent by solar gravity before arrival. Whether it will hit at all.\n4. **ASSESSMENT.** Impact risk **cannot yet be excluded**. Preliminary models place the close-approach window approximately one year out.\n5. **ACTION.** Analysis teams convene today. First position projection due in 22 days. Teams should develop tools for describing motion, predicting position, and quantifying uncertainty.\n\n*DIST: ANALYSIS TEAMS · FITCHBURG STATION — PDCO Watch Desk*\n\n> Read like an analyst: what is **stated**, what is **estimated**, what is **unknown**?"}
 ]$$::jsonb
